# the argument of euthydemus-knowing.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Euthydemus-style sophism: "knowing" shifts sense between "knowing (the things one knows)"
# and "knowing" without qualification. The sophist treats them as one; Socrates' replies (S4, S8) qualify them.
# The atom "knowing" is the sophist's unqualified sense; the qualified senses get their own atoms.
atom	knows_something	Socrates knows something
atom	ignorant_some	there are many other things which Socrates does not know
atom	knowing	Socrates is knowing (without qualification, the sophist's sense)
atom	knowing_known	Socrates is knowing of the knowledge which he has (qualified sense)
atom	not_knowing_unknown	Socrates is not knowing of that which he does not know (qualified sense)
atom	knows_all	Socrates knows all things
P1	S2	knows_something	kind empirical
P2	S3	knows_something → knowing
O1	S4	knows_something → knowing_known	against P2
# S5 "That makes no difference": the sophist denies the qualification matters
R1	S5	knowing_known → knowing	against O1
P3	S5	knowing → knows_all
P4	S6	¬knows_all
P5	S6	ignorant_some	kind empirical
P6	S7	ignorant_some → ¬knowing
O2	S8	ignorant_some → not_knowing_unknown	against P6
C	S9	knowing ∧ ¬knowing	from P1 P2 P5 P6
